# b_Sears.dat -- bound coherent neutron scattering lengths and cross sections.
#
# CITE:  Sears, V. F. (1992). Neutron News 3(3), 26-37.
#        Sears, V. F. "Scattering lengths for neutrons",
#        International Tables for Crystallography Vol. C, ch. 4.4.4,
#        Table 4.4.4.1.
#
# The second citation is the crystallographic reference of record and the one
# this package already relies on elsewhere (Vol. C is cited for dispersion in
# crystallography/dispersion.py, for flat-plate absorption and for the
# Lorentz-polarisation factor in schemas/instrument.py).
#
# OBTAINED FROM the NIST Center for Neutron Research's machine-readable copy,
# https://www.ncnr.nist.gov/resources/n-lengths/list.html (retrieved
# 2026-08-23) -- a convenience, not the reference, exactly as
# f0_WaasKirf.dat was obtained from the ESRF DABAX collection while citing
# Waasmaier & Kirfel.
#
# THERMAL VALUES ONLY. There is no energy dependence here. A few resonant
# absorbers -- Cd, Sm, Eu, Gd, and notably 113Cd and 157Gd -- have complex,
# wavelength-dependent b near a resonance, the neutron analogue of an X-ray
# absorption edge. At a single constant wavelength one thermal value per
# species is the right number; a time-of-flight bank spans a range of
# wavelengths and would need b(lambda), which this table cannot supply.
#
# b_coh may be NEGATIVE (a 180-degree phase shift on scattering, not an error):
# H, Li, Ti, V, Mn among the natural-abundance elements. |F|^2 stays positive;
# individual terms in the sum do not.
#
# b_coh depends on ISOTOPE, not only on element: b(1H) = -3.7406 fm against
# b(2H) = +6.671 fm is a sign change, which is why deuteration is routine.
# Rows whose symbol starts with a mass number are isotopes; the bare symbol is
# the natural-abundance average.
#
# "---" in the source means "not tabulated" and is written here as nan.
#
# columns: symbol  conc(%)  b_coh(fm)  b_inc(fm)  xs_coh(barn)  xs_inc(barn)  xs_scatt(barn)  xs_abs(barn)

